' ===========================================================================
' QB64.BM - what the game needs from BASIC PDS 7.1 that QB64 does not have:
' DIR$, the pixels of a dotted LINE, the names of the system it runs on,
' and silence. Included at the end of WON.BAS and
' of NAPOLEON.BAS.
' DIR$ is that of the Civil War Strategy port (Hutsell-Games/civil-war-strategy).
' ===========================================================================

' PDS 7.1's DIR$: the first file that matches spec$, or the next match of
' the last search when spec$ is "" (PDS: DIR$ with no argument). Folders are
' skipped, and the name is in upper case, as DOS gave it.
' Without this FUNCTION, QB64pe and QB64Rust read DIR$("*.sav") as an array.
FUNCTION DIR$ (spec$)
IF spec$ = "" THEN f$ = _FILES$ ELSE f$ = _FILES$(spec$)
DO WHILE RIGHT$(f$, 1) = CHR$(92) OR RIGHT$(f$, 1) = "/"
	f$ = _FILES$
LOOP
DIR$ = UCASE$(f$)
END FUNCTION

' -1 on Windows, 0 elsewhere. DOS's .EXE names and cmd's copy exist only
' there. As in the World War 2 port (Hutsell-Games/world-war-2).
FUNCTION onwindows%
onwindows% = INSTR(_OS$, "[WINDOWS]") > 0
END FUNCTION

' Silences PLAY and SOUND, which both play through voice 0, for a scripted
' run (scriptstart, napstart): a test plays the game on a hidden desktop,
' but its sound still reaches the user's speakers. Every note still takes
' its time, so nothing that a test compares changes.
SUB silence
PLAY "V0"
END SUB

' The name of a program as SHELL needs it: the strategic game starts the
' tactical game, napoleon.exe on Windows and napoleon elsewhere. Off Windows
' SHELL hands the name to the shell, which looks for a bare name on the
' PATH, not in the game's folder: hence "./".
FUNCTION exename$ (base$)
IF onwindows% THEN exename$ = base$ + ".exe" ELSE exename$ = "./" + base$
END FUNCTION

' LINE (xa, ya)-(xb, yb), c, , style& with the pixels PDS 7.1 and QB 4.5 gave.
' QB64 starts the style at the first point, and steps to the next row halfway
' along the line. The old compilers start at the left end and step earlier,
' from a first error of 3/4 of the longer side. A vertical line has no left
' end: there they start at the first point, as QB64 does, so a road drawn
' from each of its cities gets two sets of dots. Measured with QB64Rust's
' verification\qb45\q45_line_style and q45_line_pixels (DIVERGENCES-QB45.md
' Q-024 and Q-025).
SUB styledline (xa, ya, xb, yb, c, style&)
x = xa: y = ya: x2 = xb: y2 = yb
IF x > x2 THEN SWAP x, x2: SWAP y, y2
sx = SGN(x2 - x): sy = SGN(y2 - y)
ax = ABS(x2 - x): ay = ABS(y2 - y)
IF ax >= ay THEN n = ax: d = ay ELSE n = ay: d = ax
e = (3 * n) \ 4
FOR i = 0 TO n
	IF style& AND 2& ^ (15 - (i MOD 16)) THEN PSET (x, y), c
	e = e + d
	IF ax >= ay THEN
		IF e >= n AND n > 0 THEN e = e - n: y = y + sy
		x = x + sx
	ELSE
		IF e >= n THEN e = e - n: x = x + sx
		y = y + sy
	END IF
NEXT i
END SUB
